# recognition.json: how the Core reads a game's weapon names

A game folder here may hold a `recognition.json` beside its `catalog.json`. It
is what lets the Core **read the weapon's name** off the HUD, so every gun in
the catalog is recognised without the player teaching a picture of each one.
The design and the numbers are in `docs/NAME-READING.md` (D132, proposed).

**Status: on the bench.** `cod-bo2/recognition.json` exists (2026-09-24,
taught from the owner's own frames); the app does not read it yet. The Core
reads it only in a bench build (`CONFIG_ZS_TEXTREAD_BENCH`), pushed from the
PC with `firmware/lite-p4/tools/teach_bench.py name-push`; no config carries a
name area yet (D132's format is not built).

Unlike `catalog.json`, this file IS sent to a device: the app puts its font
(and its `rarity` block, when the game has one) into a config (the TEXT
section) when a config of this game watches a name.

## Who makes it

The owner, once per game, with `tools/train_textread.py`. Players never train
a font; they only draw a box around the name on their own screen.

## How to make one

1. **Screenshots.** In the game, with a gun out, take the app's screenshot
   (Screen Assist, the same one the Core serves at `GET /vision/frame`). Crop
   around the weapon's name, a little loose, and save it named for the gun:
   its catalog id (`assault_rifles.ak_27.png`) or its name (`AK-27.png`); a
   second screenshot of the same gun is `AK-27 (2).png`.
2. **Which guns.** Every letter and digit the game's names use must be seen
   at least once. Run the tool with whatever you have; it prints the
   characters still missing and the guns that would fill them. For Warzone, 10
   names cover all 39 characters; take each twice, on different backgrounds
   (sky, ground, a dark room): about 20 to 30 crops.
3. **Run it:**

   ```
   python firmware/lite-p4/components/zs_textread/tools/train_textread.py cod-warzone D:\crops\warzone
   ```

   It sets every fourth crop aside, teaches from the rest, then reads the
   set-aside ones against every name in the catalog. Read its report:
   - `not used: ... (reason)`: a crop it could not line up with its label.
     Usually a wrong label or a crop that cuts a letter off.
   - `the font lacks ...: screenshot ...`: characters still missing, and
     which guns to add.
   - `verified on N crops: R right, N none, W WRONG`. **It refuses to write a
     font that named any wrong gun.** A few `none` are fine (the Core's
     hysteresis and the per-gun Check button cover them); look at them anyway.
4. **Check it in, with the catalog.** The app picks it up with the game.

Options: `--case mixed` for a game that prints names in Mixed Case (Fortnite),
`--polarity dark` for dark text on a light plate, `--verify DIR` to check on a
separate folder instead of setting crops aside, `--blank hold` for a game whose
name fades a few seconds after a swap, `--decoy "TEXT"` for a string the HUD
shows that is no gun.

### Whole frames, for a game whose name needs a plane (Black Ops II)

Black Ops II writes its name as dark letters on see-through light plates, one
plate a word, leaning right, the gun first on a line that grows to the left
("MSMC | LONG BARREL | QUICKDRAW"), so the Core reads a PLANE made from its
own colour frame (`zs_textread_plane.h`), not the luma of a crop. Teach it
from whole screenshots, the Core's own 1280x720 frames, and a `labels.json`
beside them giving each frame's whole line as it reads (`""` when no name
shows: dead, a menu, an EMP):

```
python firmware/lite-p4/tools/teach_bench.py name-train      (the same as:)
python firmware/lite-p4/components/zs_textread/tools/train_textread.py cod-bo2 firmware/lite-p4/tools/teach
    --labels firmware/lite-p4/tools/teach/labels.json --plane plate --span words
    --max-h 24 --shear 16 --margin 0.5 --joined "DUAL WIELD" --drops 100 110 120 130
python firmware/lite-p4/tools/teach_bench.py name-offline    every labelled frame, read as the Core reads it
```

`--span words`: the gun is a run of whole words at the line's start, so the
class name or the attachments after it cost nothing. The bar between two
boxes is taught as a glyph (`|`); a line that shows none in its label
("MSMC CUSTOMIZED") is taught with one after the gun, because the gun is
always its own box, unless the rest is in the gun's box (`--joined`).

### A small, white, mixed-case name, and letters no frame showed (Black Ops 7, Warzone: D411)

Black Ops 7 writes the gun's name in white, mixed case, **7 px tall at 720p**,
right-aligned over the ammo count. At that size the reader's pieces join, so
the Core reads the box **doubled** (`--plane luma2x`: the luma upsampled 2x,
a 14-px name; `--min-h 12 --max-h 32` are plane pixels). The owner's real
frames show only some letters; `--synthetic-font default` DRAWS the others
(`synth_glyphs.py`: every catalog name in Russo One, the closest font on this
PC to the HUD's, degraded like the capture, over the world of the real
frames) and keeps every real glyph. It then holds each gun out in turn and
reads its frames (`verified`), and refuses a font that names a wrong gun:

```
cd firmware/lite-p4
python components/zs_textread/tools/train_textread.py cod-bo7 ../../docs/reference/cod-hud-names-2026-10-20
    --labels ../../docs/reference/cod-hud-names-2026-10-20/labels.json --plane luma2x --span line
    --case mixed --box 0.69 0.85 0.145 0.035 --teach-box 0.75 0.85 0.085 0.035 --min-h 12 --max-h 32
    --threshold 0.70 --margin 0.5 --hunt --synthetic-font default --synthetic-packs cod-bo7 cod-warzone
```

`labels.json` maps each frame to the name as the HUD writes it ("Jäger 45",
"M15 MOD 0"), `""` for no name, `null` for a frame not to use (a name fading
in). The README beside the frames says which frames show what.

### labels.csv, when a file name is not enough

```
file,text,item
ak.png,AK-27,                          text looked up in the catalog by name
pk.png,PEACEKEEPER MK 1,assault_rifles.peacekeeper_mk1
                                       the HUD spells it differently: recorded
                                       as that gun's HUD spelling
bp.png,SONIC BOOM,-                    a DECOY: shown by the game, no gun
menu.png,,none                         must read as nothing (a check only)
```

## Rarity: teaching the Core a game's tier colours (D134, proposed)

For a game whose catalog lists `tiers` (Warzone: Common, Uncommon, Rare, Epic,
Legendary, Loadout, Ultra), the same file carries a `rarity` block, so the
Core can tell a Legendary M15 from a Common one and run each its own Smart
Actions. The design and the numbers are in `docs/NAME-READING.md`, section 10.
The player does nothing new: the box round the name is enough. The owner
teaches the colours once per game:

1. **Where the colour is.** For Warzone it is the small icon right after the
   name (a chevron and a diamond with 0 to 4 pips; a bar and a cross on a
   loadout gun), so the place is `mark`, from an eighth of a cap height to 1.9
   cap heights after the name's end, a fifth of a cap height above and below
   it. Another game draws it elsewhere: `text` when the name itself is
   coloured, `rect` for a solid band. The place is the owner's decision from a
   screenshot; the tool checks it on every crop.
2. **The crops.** For **every tier**, a gun of that tier in hand, at least
   **three reads over different backgrounds: one over something dark or grey
   (a night map, concrete, the HUD over a shadow) and the others over colour
   (sky, grass, fire)**. The dark one teaches the tier's own colour; the
   coloured ones teach how see-through the HUD is. About 25 reads for
   Warzone's seven. They come from the Core itself: with a gun of the tier
   out, the tool asks the Core for its raw reading of the name area
   (`/vision/status` gives the sampled colour and the world's around it), so
   the tier is learned from exactly the numbers the Core will measure, through
   the owner's own capture chain. (D118's screenshot is grey; a colour
   screenshot would do too, once it exists.)
3. **The teacher** (`zs_rar_learn`, through the owner's tool) measures each
   tier's clean colour, how much of the world the HUD lets through (never
   less than 0.55, whatever it measured), and a region per tier with a gap
   between every two. **It refuses two tiers whose crops overlap in colour,
   naming both**; then take more crops of those two over a dark world, or
   decide that game cannot tell them apart.
4. **Check** on reads it did not learn from: every one must be its own tier or
   unknown, never another. Common's grey icon over clutter is often unknown;
   that is expected, and the any-rarity Smart Actions run.
5. **Check it in** with the rest of the file.

When to redo it: a HUD redesign, a new tier (the catalog's `tiers` grows),
or players reporting a tier that never reads (the per-gun Check says why:
"no icon", "the world could make it two tiers").

## Two name strips: Apex Legends (D461)

Apex prints BOTH guns' names side by side, under the weapon panel, and fills the
strip of the gun in hand with its ammo colour at about twice the brightness of
the other. `read.twin` (`{"dx": 0.0885, "ratio": 1.25}`) makes the Core read the
player's box AND the box moved `dx` of the frame to the right, and read the name
only from the strip whose median brightness is `ratio` times the other's; closer
than that it names nothing (a swap, a dark world). The blob carries it as a
trailer after the names ('T' 'W', i16 dx x 32767, u16 ratio x 1000), which a
firmware before D461 never reads (it reads the left strip only).
`train_textread.py --twin 0.0885 1.25` writes it; `tools/twin_eval.py` reads
two-strip frames held out by video. On PC the HUD draws a key cap ("1", "2") at
the left of each strip: a PC player's box leaves it out (`box.pc`).

## Hotbar icons: Rust, Fortnite (D461)

A game that prints no name (Rust) or only for two seconds (Fortnite) is read by
its held hotbar slot's ICON. Its recognition.json says `"reader": "zs_iconread 1"`
and carries an `icons` block instead of `text` and `font`:

| field | what it is |
|---|---|
| `icons.finder` | `blue`: Rust's held slot is a solid blue tile; `band`: Fortnite's sits over a white band (an outlined slot, Reload, is template set 1) |
| `icons.rows`, `pool`, `mask` | the feature: the tile at 48 x 48, its first `rows` rows, grids of `pool` x `pool` cells; mask 1 blanks Rust's condition bar and count |
| `icons.accept`, `lead` | a compare names an item at `accept` or more AND `lead` ahead of every other item |
| `icons.search` | rows and columns the tile is shifted to find the best compare |
| `icons.every` | the same slot is compared again only every this many reads (a moved slot at once) |
| `icons.items` | the items the templates name, in order: `{"id": catalog id}`, or `{"name": ..., "decoy": true}` for an item that is no catalog gun (a potion, a bow nobody could name): read, armed nothing |
| `icons.b64` | the templates as the Core receives them: u16 count, then each a name index, a set, and the feature at 4 bits a value |
| `read` | `streak` and `blank` (`hold`: the hotbar fades, the gun memory keeps the gun) |

The app builds the blob (`'Z' 'I'`, `zs_textread_icon.h`) from it exactly as it
builds a name reader's, and it rides in the config the same way (D424). It is
made by `firmware/lite-p4/components/zs_textread/tools/train_iconread.py` from
the Model Library's labelled tiles (`tools/icon_coreframes.py` cuts the Core's
720p strips), each creator held out in turn through the Core's own code, and it
refuses templates that name a wrong item on more than 1% of the strips.

## The weapon panel's text line: Counter-Strike 2 (D473)

CS2 draws every slot's icon on the right, bottom up, and prints a TEXT LINE only under the
held one (its name, skin, owner or name tag). `icons.finder: "text"` (3) finds that line in the
box (bright ink over the darkest within 2 px, letters 6 to 32 px tall at 1080p, joined into a
line that crosses at least 3 strokes in its middle rows) and takes the icon right over it,
right-aligned with it, as the tile: its own box, so any HUD colour and any HUD scale. A knife's
icon is a long thin blade, taken when it is 0.8 text heights tall and 4 wide. An item that
shares its row (the Zeus beside the knife, a grenade, the C4) has its name CENTRED under it:
with no right-aligned icon, the icon-sized piece centred nearest the line is taken. No line:
no slot (the panel fades after a swap unless "Always Show Inventory" is on; the memory holds
the gun). The suggested box is 0.80 0.62 0.20 0.38 (a long name and the C4 row fit).

`icons.mask` bit 1 (2, WIDE) resamples the tile to 48 x `rows` (CS2: 18, pool 3: 2 x 6 x 16
values) instead of 48 x 48, so a 3:1 rifle keeps its shape; a firmware before D473 refuses
finder 3 and those rows, with a sentence. Bit 2 (4, MIRROR) also matches the feature with each
grid row reversed: CS2 mirrors every HUD icon for a left-handed viewmodel (older firmware
ignores the bit and reads right-handed HUDs only). One template an item; every KNIFE model is
one decoy "Knife" and every GRENADE one decoy "Grenade" (up to 4 templates each): read, they arm
nothing and the gun's Scripts stop (a mirrored Karambit is a Talon, and the grenade row's name
is not under the held grenade, so which knife or grenade cannot be told reliably). The
finder's scratch is the name reader's steady-box history, which an icon reader never uses: no
memory added.

`round` (CS2 only) says how the round memory works for this game: the buy-phase read as the
prior, `read.streak` 3 agreeing reads before a picked-up gun replaces it, no HUD-gone forget
(after death CS2 shows the spectated teammate's HUD), and the game's default weapon keys for the
key hook (D466). App data; the Core reads only the blob.

## When to update it

- **A new season adds guns:** only `catalog.json` changes. Re-run the tool
  only if it now reports a missing character (the first gun with a Q, say).
- **The HUD changes font, size or colour** (a new title, a HUD redesign):
  take new screenshots and re-run.
- **Players report a gun that never reads:** find why with the per-gun Check
  button; a HUD spelling goes into labels.csv, an unreadable name into
  `names.unreadable` (by hand), and that gun falls back to a taught picture.

## The file

```json
{
  "zerostickRecognition": 1,
  "game": "cod-warzone",
  "trainedOn": "2026-10-01",
  "catalogAsOf": "2026-09-23",
  "reader": "zs_textread 1",
  "text": { "case": "upper", "polarity": "bright", "inkDrop": 25 },
  "font": { "bits": 2, "bytes": 3207, "shapes": "AK-27BOXING...", "b64": "WlQB..." },
  "read": { "threshold": 0.70, "margin": 0.25, "streak": 3, "blank": "none" },
  "names": { "hud": {}, "unreadable": [], "decoys": ["SONIC BOOM"] },
  "missing": "'",
  "verified": { "crops": 65, "right": 65, "none": 0, "wrong": 0 },
  "training": { "crops": 28, "used": 28 },
  "rarity": {
    "tiers": ["common", "uncommon", "rare", "epic", "legendary", "loadout", "ultra"],
    "place": { "where": "mark", "from": "right", "x0": 2, "x1": 31, "y0": -3, "y1": 19, "share": 15 },
    "mix": [0, 55],
    "read": { "minSamples": 12, "agree": 60, "spread": 24, "streak": 3, "unknown": "hold" },
    "b64": "WlIBBwIDAh8...",
    "verified": { "reads": 60, "right": 51, "unknown": 9, "wrong": 0 },
    "training": { "reads": 42, "used": 36 }
  }
}
```

| field | what it is |
|---|---|
| `text.case` | `upper` or `mixed`: how the HUD prints names (room below the baseline) |
| `text.polarity` | `bright` text on the world, or `dark` text on a light plate |
| `text.inkDrop` | chosen by the tool on your own crops (how far a stroke's edge may fall below the text's core brightness) |
| `text.plane` | what the Core reads: `luma` (the default), `ink`, `plate` or `luma2x` (luma upsampled 2x for a name about 7 px tall, D411; a firmware before D411 refuses it and says so) (`zs_textread_plane.h`) |
| `text.minCapHeight` | the shortest cap height read, plane pixels (written by `--synthetic-font`; the font carries it) |
| `text.span` | `line`: the box holds the name alone; `words`: the name is the first words of a longer line |
| `text.shear64` | the italic's lean taken out before reading, 64ths of a pixel a row (Black Ops II: 16) |
| `text.maxCapHeight` | the tallest cap height read, capture pixels; bounds the plate search |
| `box.suggested` | where the name sits on the owner's screen, 0..1 (the bench push uses it) |
| `font.b64` | the learned font, byte for byte what the Core receives (layout in `zs_textread.h`, "the wire") |
| `font.shapes` | every character shape the font learned, case kept |
| `font.real`, `font.drawn`, `font.drawnTwins` | D411: the shapes learned from real frames, the shapes DRAWN because no real frame showed them, and real shapes that also keep a drawn twin (their real samples disagreed with the drawing). A drawn glyph has samples 0 on the wire; `font.realSamples` counts each real one, `font.drawnFrom` says how they were drawn |
| `read.threshold`, `read.margin` | how sure a read must be, and how far ahead of every other name (in characters) |
| `read.streak` | reads in a row before the answer changes (D119's minHits) |
| `read.blank` | `none`: an empty box lets go (D119); `hold`: an empty box keeps the last gun, for names that fade |
| `read.hunt` | `true`: when the player's box names nothing, the Core looks over the bottom-right of the bottom quarter for the name (the firing range, a HUD laid out differently), moves its box there for the session, and says what the screen is (in a match, firing range, menu, no HUD). Call of Duty packs; `train_textread.py --hunt` writes it (docs/NAME-READING.md section 13) |
| `names.hud` | catalog id to the spelling the HUD uses, where they differ |
| `names.unreadable` | catalog ids the Core cannot identify by name; they go straight to a taught picture |
| `names.decoys` | strings the HUD shows that are no gun; a read lands on them and arms nothing |
| `missing` | characters the catalog uses that the font never saw; names holding them can still be read, just never confirmed by that letter |
| `verified` | the tool's check on crops it did not learn from (`--synthetic-font`: every gun held out in turn, `perGun`) |
| `rarity` | optional, for a game whose catalog lists `tiers` (D134): how the Core reads the gun's tier |
| `rarity.tiers` | the catalog's tier ids in the catalog's order; the Core only ever sees their indices |
| `rarity.place` | where the colour is, in sixteenths of the name's cap height from its `left` or `right` end and from its cap line: `mark` (an icon: the pixels that stand out most, `share` percent of the rectangle at most), `text` (the name's own letters) or `rect` (a solid band) |
| `rarity.mix` | how much of the world the HUD lets through, percent, measured by the teacher; the upper bound is never under 55 |
| `rarity.read` | how many samples a read needs, how many of them must agree, the reads in a row before the tier changes, and whether an unknown read keeps the tier (`hold`) or counts towards letting go (`none`, D119's rule) |
| `rarity.b64` | the whole block as the Core receives it (`zs_rar_pack`, `zs_textread_rarity.h` "the wire"): 17 bytes and 6 a tier, 59 for Warzone, the learned colour regions included |
| `rarity.verified`, `rarity.training` | the tool's reads, as for the font |
